<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Peter's Basilica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Peter's Basilica is a church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> built over the traditional tomb of the apostle Peter. In the old basilica on this site, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> emperor on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 800 CE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> treat the coronation as the founding moment of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica.jpg" alt="St. Peter's Basilica" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alvesgaspar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Old St. Peter's Basilica was the fourth-century church begun by the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> between 319 and 333. It had a wide nave, two aisles on each side and an apsidal end, and it was over 103 metres long, with a large colonnaded atrium. It was built over a small shrine believed to mark the burial place of Saint Peter, and it held most of the popes from Peter to the 15th century. By the end of the 15th century, after neglect during the Avignon Papacy, it had fallen into disrepair.</p><p>Pope Nicholas V, who reigned from 1447 to 1455, was the first to consider rebuilding, but little was achieved before his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> then held a competition, and the design of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Donato Bramante</a> was chosen: a Greek cross crowned by a dome inspired by the Pantheon. Construction began on 18 April 1506. One method of financing it was granting indulgences in return for contributions, promoted by Albrecht, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, who appointed the Dominican preacher Johann Tetzel; his salesmanship provoked a scandal and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> took over a building site where four enormous piers were rising behind the remaining nave of the old basilica. The dome rises to a total height of 136.57 metres from the floor to the top of the external cross, with an internal diameter of 41.47 metres. On 18 February 1606, under Pope Paul V, the dismantling of the remaining parts of the Constantinian basilica began. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-maderno">Carlo Maderno</a> added the nave and façade, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> increasingly saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cross as the true symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. The basilica was completed on 18 November 1626.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a> received the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> in 1626 and worked on the basilica for 50 years; his works include the baldachin and the chair of Saint Peter, and he designed St. Peter's Square, built between 1656 and 1667. Catholic tradition holds that the tomb of Saint Peter lies directly below the high altar. The dome greatly influenced church architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Western Christendom</a>, from Sant'Andrea della Valle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Christopher Wren's dome at St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Peter's Basilica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Christmas Day 800 CE, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in <b>St. Peter's Basilica</b> in Rome, where he was acclaimed Imperator Romanorum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Christmas Mass of 800, in the old basilica on the site of <b>St. Peter's</b> in Rome, Pope Leo III crowned the Frankish king Charlemagne, who was acclaimed Imperator Romanorum, Emperor of the Romans.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Adeste Fideles&quot; is traditionally the final anthem of Midnight Mass at <b>St. Peter's Basilica</b> in the Vatican.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>The state covers about 44 hectares around <b>Saint Peter's Basilica</b> and the Apostolic Palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/vatican-city">Vatican City</a></li></ul></section>
